            <section class="how" id="how">
                <div class="wrap">
                    <header>
                        <p class="eyebrow">How it works</p>
                        <h2>One self-contained library per engine.</h2>
                        <p>
                            Each engine is built into a single shared library with its own ggml linked in statically and kept private.
                            The engines pin different ggml versions, and this is what lets several of them share one process.
                        </p>
                    </header>
                    <div class="row">
                        <article class="feature"><h3>stable-diffusion.cpp</h3><p>Has a key/value C API, so <code>fornax-sd</code> binds it directly.</p></article>
                        <article class="feature"><h3>llama.cpp</h3><p>Its C API passes structs by value and is mid-migration to a new batch API, so <code>fornax-llama</code> binds a small, stable C layer instead.</p></article>
                        <article class="feature"><h3>acestep.cpp and yue2.cpp</h3><p>C++ only, so each gets a C layer of about a hundred lines: a JSON request in, PCM audio out.</p></article>
                        <article class="feature"><h3>Tool calling</h3><p><code>LlamaModel.chatJson</code> takes messages and tools in OpenAI’s format and returns a message with <code>content</code>, <code>reasoning_content</code> and <code>tool_calls</code>. It uses the model’s own chat template and tool-call format, with the same templates and parsers as llama-server.</p></article>
                        <article class="feature"><h3>ModelPool</h3><p>Keeps models resident between uses, the way ComfyUI does. It unloads idle models least recently used first to stay within a memory budget, can unload after an idle timeout, and gives each model one user at a time.</p></article>
                        <article class="feature"><h3>Pick a backend at build time</h3><p>The Java is the same for every backend. <code>native/build.sh</code> builds the libraries for the one you name.</p></article>
                        <article class="feature"><h3>Long jobs on small power budgets</h3><p>A long GPU dispatch can trip a laptop driver’s compute timeout, so ACE-Step audio is decoded in 512-frame tiles. Tune it with <code>FORNAX_ACESTEP_VAE_CHUNK</code>.</p></article>
                        <article class="feature"><h3>The engines’ own logs</h3><p>Set <code>FORNAX_VERBOSE=1</code> to see what llama.cpp, stable-diffusion.cpp and friends print.</p></article>
                    </div>
                </div>
            </section>

